var definitions = ['<h2>Je ne sais pas si je veux porter celui-ci ou celui-là.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>ne</strong> This is a negation particle in French. It’s used in conjunction with "pas" to make a sentence negative. However, in informal spoken French, it is often dropped.</div><div><strong>sais</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "savoir", which means "to know". In this sentence, it signifies the speaker’s lack of knowledge or uncertainty.</div><div><strong>pas</strong> This is another negation particle that, when used with "ne", negates the verb. It translates to "not" in English.</div><div><strong>si</strong> This word means "if" in English. It introduces a conditional clause.</div><div><strong>veux</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "vouloir", which means "to want". It indicates the speaker’s desire or lack thereof.</div><div><strong>porter</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb that means "to wear". It is the action that is being considered by the speaker.</div><div><strong>celui-ci</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun meaning "this one" in English. It refers to something close to the speaker.</div><div><strong>ou</strong> This is the conjunction for "or" in English. It’s used to show a choice or alternative between two options.</div><div><strong>celui-là</strong> This is another demonstrative pronoun meaning "that one" in English. It refers to something further away from the speaker.</div>','<h2>Les deux voitures sont belles, mais je veux celle-là.</h2><div><strong>Les</strong> This is the French definite article for plural nouns, equivalent to "the" in English. It precedes the noun "voitures" (cars).</div><div><strong>deux</strong> This is the French cardinal number meaning "two". It quantifies the noun "voitures", indicating there are two of them.</div><div><strong>voitures</strong> This is the plural form of the French noun "voiture", which means "car" in English.</div><div><strong>sont</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It links the subject "les deux voitures" to their description.</div><div><strong>belles</strong> This is the plural form of the French adjective "beau" (feminine "belle"), meaning "beautiful" or "handsome" in English. It agrees in gender and number with the noun "voitures" and describes the cars as beautiful.</div><div><strong>mais</strong> This is the French word for "but". It is a conjunction used to introduce a contrast or exception.</div><div><strong>je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the clause.</div><div><strong>veux</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "vouloir", which means "to want". It reflects the speaker’s desire or wish regarding the object.</div><div><strong>celle-là</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun in French meaning "that one" in English. It refers to a specific noun, in this case, one of the two cars mentioned previously. "Celle" is the feminine singular form because "voiture" is feminine, and "là" indicates "that" as opposed to "this" (which would be "ci").</div>','<h2>J’aime ceux-ci.</h2><div><strong>J’</strong> This is a contraction of the French word "Je", which means "I" in English. The contraction is used before a vowel or mute h for ease of pronunciation. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>aime</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "aimer", which means "to like" or "to love" in English. It describes the action that the subject is taking.</div><div><strong>ceux-ci</strong> This is a French demonstrative pronoun that means "these ones" or "these" in English. It is the masculine plural form and refers to a specific group of masculine or mixed objects or people that are close by. If referring to feminine objects or people, it would be "celles-ci".</div>','<h2>J’aimerais ceux-ci et ceux-là.</h2><div><strong>J’aimerais</strong> This is the first-person singular conditional tense of the verb "aimer", which means "to like" or "to love". Here it conveys a polite desire, equivalent to "I would like" in English.</div><div><strong>ceux-ci</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun in French, meaning "these ones" (for masculine plural nouns). It is used to refer to a specific set of items near the speaker.</div><div><strong>et</strong> This word translates to "and" in English. It’s a conjunction used to connect words or groups of words.</div><div><strong>ceux-là</strong> Similar to "ceux-ci", this is another demonstrative pronoun meaning "those ones" (for masculine plural nouns). It refers to items that are further away from the speaker.</div>','<h2>Qui a fait ce désordre? Qui a fait cela?</h2><div><strong>Qui</strong> This is the French word for "who". It is used to ask about the identity of a person or persons involved in an action.</div><div><strong>a fait</strong> This is the third-person singular past tense of the verb "faire", which means "to do" or "to make". In this context, it’s used in the passé composé, which is a compound past tense. "a" is the auxiliary verb "avoir" in the present tense, and "fait" is the past participle of "faire".</div><div><strong>ce</strong> This is a demonstrative adjective meaning "this" in English. It is used to point out a specific thing or situation.</div><div><strong>désordre?</strong> This word translates to "mess" or "disorder" in English. In this question, it refers to the mess that was made.</div><div><strong>cela?</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun meaning "that" in English. Here, it is used instead of repeating "désordre", thus referring back to the mess mentioned in the previous question.</div>','<h2>Donne-lui ce livre. Donne-lui ceci.</h2><div>  <strong>Donne</strong> This is the imperative form of the French verb "donner", which means "to give". It is used here to command or request the action of giving. The imperative form is used without a pronoun subject.</div><div>  <strong>-lui</strong> This is an indirect object pronoun meaning "to him" or "to her" in French. It is connected to the verb "donne" with a hyphen in imperative commands to indicate the recipient of the action.</div><div>  <strong>ce</strong> This is a demonstrative adjective in French that means "this". It precedes and agrees with the masculine singular noun "livre".</div><div>  <strong>livre</strong> This is a masculine singular noun in French meaning "book".</div><div>  <strong>Donne-lui</strong> Same as described above, this continues the command to give, addressing the action to "him" or "her".</div><div>  <strong>ceci</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun in French meaning "this". It stands alone and is used to refer to an object or idea that has been mentioned or is common knowledge, or that is about to be specified.</div>','<h2>Je ne veux pas ceci, je veux cela.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>ne</strong> This is part of the negation in French. Together with "pas", it negates a verb, expressing "not" or "do not".</div><div><strong>veux</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "vouloir", which means "to want". It indicates a desire or a wish.</div><div><strong>pas</strong> This is the second part of the negation in French. Along with "ne", it negates the action of the verb, here completing the meaning of "do not want".</div><div><strong>ceci</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun in French that means "this". It’s used to point to something close to the speaker.</div><div><strong>je veux</strong> Repeating the subject pronoun "je" and the verb "veux" from earlier, which again means "I want".</div><div><strong>cela</strong> This is another demonstrative pronoun in French that means "that". It’s used to point to something further away from the speaker.</div>','<h2>Prenez ceci, vous vous sentirez mieux rapidement.</h2><div><strong>Prenez</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural imperative form of the French verb "prendre", which means "to take". In this sentence, it’s a command or suggestion addressed to "you" (singular formal or plural).</div><div><strong>ceci</strong> This is a pronoun that means "this" in English. It refers to an object, idea, or situation that is being given or demonstrated to the listener.</div><div><strong>vous</strong> This first "vous" is the same second-person formal or plural pronoun, used here as a direct object pronoun, receiving the action of "prendre". It can mean "yourself" as well as "you".</div><div><strong>vous sentirez</strong> This is the second-person singular future tense form of the verb "sentir", which means "to feel". In this context, it implies that "you will feel" something in the future.</div><div><strong>mieux</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "better". It describes the manner in which the subject will feel.</div><div><strong>rapidement</strong> This is an adverb meaning "quickly" in English. It describes the rate at which improvement in feeling or healing will occur.</div>','<h2>Qui a fait ça?</h2><div><strong>Qui</strong> This is the French word for "who". It is used to ask about the identity of a person or persons. It is the subject of the question.</div><div><strong>a</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". In this case, it is used as an auxiliary verb to form the compound past.</div><div><strong>fait</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "faire", which means "to do" or "to make". Combined with the auxiliary verb "a", it forms the past tense, asking what has been done.</div><div><strong>ça</strong> This is the French word for "that" or "it". It is a neutral pronoun referring to an action or thing mentioned or implied in the conversation.</div><div><em>The sentence "Qui a fait ça?" translates to "Who did that?" in English, and it is a question inquiring about the perpetrator or cause of an action or event.</em></div>','<h2>Donne-moi ça!</h2><div><strong>Donne</strong> This is the imperative form of the French verb "donner", which means "to give". In this sentence, it’s used as a command or request directed at the listener, meaning "Give".</div><div><strong>moi</strong> This is the French object pronoun for "me". In the imperative mood, it’s used after the verb to indicate that the speaker is the recipient of the action.</div><div><strong>ça</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun in French, equivalent to "this" or "that" in English. It’s used to refer to a thing or an idea that has been mentioned previously, or that is obvious from the context.</div>','<h2>Je ne veux pas ça.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>ne</strong> This is part of the negation in French. When paired with "pas", it negates the verb, similar to "not" in English. In spoken French, "ne" is often dropped, leaving just "pas" for negation.</div><div><strong>veux</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "vouloir", which means "to want". It indicates desire or intention.</div><div><strong>pas</strong> This is the second part of the negation in French. Together with "ne", it negates the verb. It is the equivalent of "not" in the sentence structure "do not" in English.</div><div><strong>ça</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun in French, equivalent to "that" or "it" in English. It’s used to refer to a previously mentioned or implied object or idea.</div>','<h2>Les enfants s’amusent beaucoup avec ça.</h2><div><strong>Les</strong> This is the French definite article for plural nouns, equivalent to "the" in English. It specifies a certain group of children in question.</div><div><strong>enfants</strong> This is the French word for "children". It is a plural noun referring to a group of young people.</div><div><strong>s’amusent</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the reflexive verb "s’amuser", which means "to have fun" or "to enjoy oneself". In this form, it indicates that the children are having fun or enjoying themselves.</div><div><strong>beaucoup</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "a lot" or "much". Here, it describes the extent to which the children are having fun.</div><div><strong>avec</strong> This is the French preposition meaning "with". It’s used to indicate an accompaniment or an addition.</div><div><strong>ça</strong> This is the French demonstrative pronoun, short for "cela", and translates to "that" in English. It refers to something already mentioned or easily identified in the context.</div>'];var defBonus = ['<h2>D’accord, je vais prendre celui-là.</h2><div><strong>D’accord</strong> This French expression means "okay" or "agreed". It is used to express agreement or acceptance.</div><div><strong>je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>vais</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "aller", which means "to go". In this context, it’s helping to form the near future tense, indicating an intention or decision.</div><div><strong>prendre</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb meaning "to take" in English. It’s used here in conjunction with "vais" to express a future action. Together, "je vais prendre" means "I am going to take" or "I will take".</div><div><strong>celui-là</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun in French referring to a masculine singular noun that has been previously mentioned or is obvious from context. It translates to "that one" in English.</div>','<h2>Celui-là est vraiment confortable...</h2><div><strong>Celui-là</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun in French that means "that one" referring to a masculine noun. It is used to point out a specific item that is usually physically or contextually distant from the speaker.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be" in English. It’s used here to indicate the state or quality of the subject being referred to by the demonstrative pronoun.</div><div><strong>vraiment</strong> This is an adverb meaning "really" or "truly" in English. It’s used to intensify or emphasize the adjective that follows it.</div><div><strong>confortable</strong> This is an adjective meaning "comfortable" in English. It describes a state of comfort or ease regarding the subject mentioned. In this sentence, it aligns with the masculine singular form to agree with the noun it is describing.</div>','<h2>Ah je vois? Je vais prendre celle-là.</h2><!-- "Ah je vois?" part of the sentence --><div><strong>Ah</strong> This is an interjection in French often used to express realization or understanding, similar to "Ah" or "Oh" in English.</div><div><strong>je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s used here together with the verb to indicate the action performed by the speaker.</div><div><strong>vois?</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "voir", which means "to see". In a question, it can suggest understanding or realization, as in "I see?" or "Do I understand correctly?"</div><!-- "Je vais prendre celle-là". part of the sentence --><div><strong>Je</strong> (repeated) This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English and is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>vais</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "aller", which means "to go". In this context, combined with another verb, it forms a near-future tense construction to express the speaker’s intention to do something.</div><div><strong>prendre</strong> This is an infinitive verb meaning "to take". Here, it is used to specify the action that the speaker intends to perform in the near future.</div><div><strong>celle-là</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun in French that means "that one" when referring to a feminine singular noun. It is used to indicate a specific object that is physically or contextually distant from the speaker.</div>','<h2>Je n’aime pas celui-là non plus.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>n’aime</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense negative form of the verb "aimer", which means "to like" or "to love". In negative sentences, "ne" precedes the verb and another negative word follows it. Here, "ne" is contracted to "n’" because the verb begins with a vowel. The complete negative structure is "ne...pas", meaning "not" in English.</div><div><strong>pas</strong> This is part of the negation that follows the verb in the sentence and means "not". Together with "ne" (or "n’" in this case), they negate the verb "aimer".</div><div><strong>celui-là</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun in French, equivalent to "that one" in English. It points out a specific item or person that has likely already been mentioned.</div><div><strong>non plus</strong> This is a French phrase that means "neither" or "either" in negative constructions. It’s used at the end of the sentence here to indicate that the speaker does not like "that one" either, implying a comparison with something else that was previously discussed.</div>','<h2>Ah? Elle est bonne celle-là? Tu aimes les nez?</h2><div><strong>Ah?</strong> This is an interjection used in French to express surprise or to ask for confirmation. It’s similar to "Oh?" or "Eh?" in English.</div><div><strong>Elle</strong> This is the third-person singular feminine pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" or "it" in English, depending on the context. Here, it refers to a situation or a joke previously mentioned.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be".</div><div><strong>bonne</strong> This is the feminine singular form of the adjective "bon", meaning "good" in English. In this context, it’s used to mean "funny" (referring to a joke) or "good" (referring to a situation).</div><div><strong>celle-là</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun meaning "that one" in English. It refers to something that has just been mentioned or is understood in the context.</div><div><strong>Tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>aimes</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "aimer", which means "to like" or "to love" in English.</div><div><strong>les</strong> This is the definite article in French, used here as plural, and translates to "the" in English.</div><div><strong>nez</strong> This is the plural form of the noun "nez", which means "nose" in English.</div><div>The entire sentence, "<em>Ah? Elle est bonne celle-là? Tu aimes les nez?</em>", could be understood as an incredulous or teasing question in a conversational context, potentially after someone has made a joke or comment about noses, suggesting "Oh? That one’s good, isn’t it? You like noses?"</div>','<h2>Je ne sais pas si je veux porter celui-ci ou celui-là.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>ne...pas</strong> This is the negation used in French to turn a statement into its negative form. In English, it corresponds to the word "not".</div><div><strong>sais</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "savoir", which means "to know". Here, it refers to the fact that the speaker does not know.</div><div><strong>si</strong> In English, "si" translates to "if". It introduces a conditional clause in the sentence.</div><div><strong>veux</strong> This word is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "vouloir", which means "to want". It indicates the speaker’s desire or lack thereof.</div><div><strong>porter</strong> This is the French infinitive verb meaning "to wear". In this sentence, it is indicating the action that the speaker may or may not want to take.</div><div><strong>celui-ci</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun in French which means "this one". It refers to a specific male item that is close to the speaker.</div><div><strong>ou</strong> This is the French word for "or". It’s a conjunction used to connect alternatives or choices.</div><div><strong>celui-là</strong> This demonstrative pronoun means "that one" in English. Similar to "celui-ci", it refers to a specific male item but it is used to point out an item that is further away from the speaker.</div>','<h2>Je ne veux pas ceci, je veux cela.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of both clauses in the sentence.</div><div><strong>ne</strong> This is a negation particle used in French to form the negative. It is part of the negation "ne...pas" which is used to negate a verb, similar to "not" in English.</div><div><strong>veux</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "vouloir", which means "to want". It indicates desire or will.</div><div><strong>pas</strong> This is the second part of the negation structure "ne...pas" used to negate a statement. When paired with "ne", it negates the verb it surrounds, in this case "veux".</div><div><strong>ceci</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun meaning "this" in English. It is used to refer to something that is near the speaker.</div><div><strong>je veux</strong> Here, "je" is repeated along with "veux" as part of the second clause, reiterating "I want" in English, leading to another statement of desire.</div><div><strong>cela</strong> This is another demonstrative pronoun meaning "that" in English. It is used to refer to something that is not near the speaker, often opposed to "ceci" which refers to something close by.</div>','<h2>Elle est venue de très loin pour voir ceci.</h2><div><strong>Elle</strong> This is the French third-person singular feminine pronoun, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". In this sentence, it is used as an auxiliary verb to form the compound past tense (passé composé).</div><div><strong>venue</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "venir", which means "to come". Used with the auxiliary verb "est", it forms the passé composé, denoting that the action has been completed in the past. The form "venue" agrees in gender and number with the feminine subject "elle".</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a French preposition which, in this context, translates to "from" in English. It is used to indicate the origin or starting point of an action or movement.</div><div><strong>très</strong> This is an adverb meaning "very" in English. It is used to intensify the adjective that follows.</div><div><strong>loin</strong> This is an adverb meaning "far" in English. It describes the distance from which "she" has come.</div><div><strong>pour</strong> This is a preposition meaning "for" in English. It indicates the purpose of the action.</div><div><strong>voir</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb meaning "to see" in English. It indicates the action she has come far to do.</div><div><strong>ceci</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun meaning "this" in English. It refers to the thing or event that she has come to see.</div>','<h2>Donne-lui ce livre. Donne-lui ceci.</h2><div><strong>Donne</strong> This is the imperative form of the French verb "donner", which means "to give". In this context, it’s a command or instruction to give.</div><div><strong>-lui</strong> This is an indirect object pronoun that means "to him" or "to her" in English. When attached to the verb in the imperative form, it indicates who should be given the item in question.</div><div><strong>ce</strong> This is a demonstrative adjective in French meaning "this". It is used here to specify which book should be given.</div><div><strong>livre</strong> This is a French noun meaning "book". It is the direct object of the sentence, the thing being given.</div><div><strong>Donne-lui</strong> As explained previously, "Donne" is the imperative form of "donner", and "-lui" is the indirect object pronoun attached to it, maintaining the command to give to him or her.</div><div><strong>ceci</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun in French meaning "this". It stands alone as the object, without being followed by a noun, typically referring to something that was mentioned before or is understood from the context.</div>','<h2>Oh, tu vois. Ceci n’est pas la bonne réponse...</h2></div><div><strong>Oh</strong> This is an interjection in French, used to express a range of emotions such as surprise, pain, or realization. It’s similar to "oh" in English.</div><div><strong>tu</strong> This is the second-person singular subject pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when addressing one person in an informal context.</div><div><strong>vois</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "voir", which means "to see". It indicates that the subject ("tu") is seeing or noticing something at the moment of speaking.</div><div><strong>Ceci</strong> This demonstrative pronoun in French means "this". It is used to refer to something that is close to the speaker in terms of distance or within the context of the conversation.</div><div><strong>n’est</strong> This is a contraction of "ne" (a negation particle) and "est" (the third-person singular present tense of the verb "être", meaning "to be"). Together, they form the negative phrase "is not" in English.</div><div><strong>pas</strong> This is a French adverb that usually combines with "ne" to form a negative construction, equivalent to "not" in English.</div><div><strong>la</strong> This is the definite article for feminine singular nouns in French, equivalent to "the" in English.</div><div><strong>bonne</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "good" or "right". In the feminine singular form, it matches with "réponse" to mean "right" or "correct" in the context of this sentence.</div><div><strong>réponse</strong> This is a feminine singular noun in French that means "answer" or "response".</div>','<h2>L’art a sa valeur créative. Le prix reflète cela.</h2><div><strong>L’art</strong> This is the French word for "the art" with "L’" being the contraction of "le" (the) before a vowel or mute "h". It denotes the subject and concept being discussed.</div><div><strong>a</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". In this context, it links "l’art" (the art) with its "valeur créative" (creative value).</div><div><strong>sa</strong> This is a possessive adjective, which means "his, her, its" in English. In this sentence, it refers to art’s own creative value.</div><div><strong>valeur créative</strong> This phrase combines "valeur" meaning "value" with "créative" meaning "creative". As a whole, "valeur créative" refers to the intrinsic creative worth or merit of art.</div><div><strong>Le prix</strong> "Le" is the French definite article "the", and "prix" means "price". Together, "Le prix" refers to the price or cost of the art.</div><div><strong>reflète</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "refléter", meaning "to reflect". It suggests that the price mirrors or represents the value mentioned earlier.</div><div><strong>cela</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun meaning "that" in English. It refers back to the subject’s "valeur créative" (creative value).</div>','<h2>C’est incroyable que tu caches cela à ton mari.</h2><div><strong>C’est</strong> This is a contraction of "ce" (it) and "est" (is), the third-person singular of the verb "être" meaning "to be". The phrase "C’est" translates to "It is" or "This is" in English. Here, it serves as the introductory statement.</div><div><strong>incroyable</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "unbelievable" or "incredible". It describes something that is hard to believe.</div><div><strong>que</strong> This is a conjunction that translates to "that" in English. It’s used to introduce a dependent clause "tu caches cela à ton mari".</div><div><strong>tu</strong> This word is the second-person singular subject pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when speaking informally to one person.</div><div><strong>caches</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense of the verb "cacher", which means "to hide". It indicates an action performed by the subject "tu".</div><div><strong>cela</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun meaning "that" or "this". It is used to refer to a specific thing or situation being discussed.</div><div><strong>à</strong> This is a preposition that can translate to "to" or "at" in English. In this sentence, it’s used before "ton mari" to indicate the indirect object of the verb "caches".</div><div><strong>ton</strong> This is a possessive adjective meaning "your". It indicates that something belongs to the person addressed, in this case, "mari" (husband).</div><div><strong>mari</strong> This is a French noun meaning "husband". It’s the direct object of the action "caches cela", thus, the person to whom the speaker is hiding something.</div>','<h2>Fabuleux. Est-ce que ça mène au balcon?</h2><div><strong>Fabuleux</strong> This is the French word for "fabulous" or "fantastic". It is an adjective usually describing something extraordinary or amazing.</div><div><strong>Est-ce que</strong> This is a French phrase used to indicate a question. It translates to "Is it that" in English and is often used to turn a statement into a yes/no question.</div><div><strong>ça</strong> This is the French demonstrative pronoun for "that" or "it". It’s used to refer to something previously mentioned or understood from context.</div><div><strong>mène</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "mener", which means "to lead". In this context, it is asking if something leads to a particular place.</div><div><strong>au</strong> This is a contraction of the preposition "à" (to) and the definite article "le" (the), making "au". It is used before masculine singular nouns and translates to "to the" in English.</div><div><strong>balcon</strong> This is a French noun meaning "balcony". It refers to an outdoor extension of a building, typically enclosed by a railing or balustrade, accessible from one or more rooms.</div>','<h2>Je dois terminer ça pour lundi prochain.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>dois</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "devoir", which means "to have to" or "must". It indicates an obligation or a necessity.</div><div><strong>terminer</strong> This is an infinitive verb in French, which means "to finish" or "to complete" in English. It’s used here to describe the action that needs to be carried out.</div><div><strong>ça</strong> This is the demonstrative pronoun in French, meaning "that" in English. It’s used to refer to an object or matter that has been previously mentioned or is understood in the context.</div><div><strong>pour</strong> This is a preposition in French that can mean "for" in English. In this sentence, it is used to indicate a deadline or a point in time by which the action needs to be completed.</div><div><strong>lundi</strong> This is a noun in French meaning "Monday" in English. It specifies the day of the week.</div><div><strong>prochain</strong> This is an adjective in French, meaning "next" in English. When used with a day of the week, it indicates the following occurrence of that day.</div>','<h2>Tu as généralement réponse à tout, mais je pense que tu as tort sur ça.</h2><div><strong>Tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when speaking informally or to one person. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>as</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". In this context, it is used to indicate possession or the state of having something.</div><div><strong>généralement</strong> This is an adverb in French that means "generally" or "usually". It modifies the verb phrase, suggesting that the action is typical or common.</div><div><strong>réponse</strong> This is a French noun meaning "answer" or "response". It is singular and feminine, and in this sentence, it follows "as" as part of a fixed expression "avoir réponse à" meaning "to have an answer for".</div><div><strong>à tout</strong> The preposition "à" translates to "to" or "at" in English, and "tout" means "everything". Combined, the phrase "à tout" means "to everything" or "for everything", indicating that the subject has a response for every question or topic.</div><div><strong>mais</strong> This is a French conjunction that means "but". It introduces a contrast or exception to what has been previously stated.</div><div><strong>je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the next clause in the sentence.</div><div><strong>pense</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "penser", which means "to think". It expresses the speaker’s opinion or belief.</div><div><strong>que</strong> This word is a conjunction in French that means "that". It’s used to introduce a subordinate clause, which in this case is expressing the speaker’s thought.</div><div><strong>tu as</strong> This is the repetition of the second-person singular present tense form of "avoir". Here, it’s leading into the speaker’s opinion about the other person’s state of being mistaken.</div><div><strong>tort</strong> This is a French noun meaning "wrong" or "mistake". In the expression "avoir tort", it means "to be wrong" or "to be mistaken".</div><div><strong>sur ça</strong> The preposition "sur" translates to "on" or "about" in English, and "ça" means "this" or "that". Combined, "sur ça" means "about that" or "on this" and refers to the specific subject that the speaker believes the other person is mistaken about.</div>','<h2>Ça va me prendre du temps.</h2><div><strong>Ça</strong> This is a pronoun in French that stands for "it" or "this/that", often used as a subject of a sentence. It is a contraction of "cela", which means "that".</div><div><strong>va</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "aller", which means "to go". In this context, it’s part of a common expression "ça va" meaning "it goes", which is used to indicate how something is proceeding or will proceed.</div><div><strong>me</strong> This is a first-person singular pronoun in the indirect object form, equivalent to "me" in English. It indicates that the action of the verb is done to or affects the speaker.</div><div><strong>prendre</strong> This verb means "to take" in English. In this context, it refers to requiring or needing something, which in this case is time.</div><div><strong>du</strong> This is a partitive article in French, which is formed from the preposition "de" (of/from) and the masculine singular definite article "le". "Du" is used before a masculine noun that is not specific or is a part of a whole. In English, it’s akin to "some" or "any".</div><div><strong>temps</strong> This means "time" in English. It’s a masculine noun and in this sentence, it’s what will be taken or required.</div>','<h2>D’accord. J’aime mieux ça!</h2><div><strong>D’accord</strong> This is a French expression meaning "okay" or "alright". It indicates agreement or consent to something that has been said or proposed.</div><div><strong>J’aime</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "aimer", which means "to like" or "to love". In this context, it means "I prefer" or "I like better".</div><div><strong>mieux</strong> This is the comparative form of "bien" in French, which means "good" or "well". "Mieux" means "better". It’s used here to indicate a preference for an alternative or to express that something is more desirable.</div><div><strong>ça</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun in French, meaning "that". It refers to something that has been mentioned or is understood from the context.</div>','<h2>Où est-ce que tu vas comme ça?</h2><div><strong>Où</strong> This is a French word for "where". It is used to ask about location or direction.</div><div><strong>est-ce que</strong> This is a phrase that is often used to form a question in French. It literally translates to "is it that" but in this context, it means "do" as part of the question structure.</div><div><strong>tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It is informal and used when addressing one person familiarly.</div><div><strong>vas</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "aller", which means "to go". Here, it describes the action of going.</div><div><strong>comme ça</strong> This French phrase translates to "like that" or "this way" in English. It is used here to ask about the manner or purpose of going somewhere, suggesting surprise or curiosity about the appearance or situation of the person being addressed.</div>','<h2>Oui! Ça pourrait être vraiment amusant.</h2><div><strong>Oui!</strong> This is the French word for "yes". It is used to express agreement or affirmation.</div><div><strong>Ça</strong> This is a contraction of "cela", meaning "that" or "it" in English. It’s a demonstrative pronoun often used to refer to something previously mentioned or easily identifiable in context.</div><div><strong>pourrait</strong> This is the third-person singular conditional tense form of the verb "pouvoir", which means "to be able to" or "can". It suggests something that might be possible or could happen under certain conditions.</div><div><strong>être</strong> This is an infinitive form of the verb "être", meaning "to be" in French. It is used here with "pourrait" to form a conditional tense phrase meaning "could be".</div><div><strong>vraiment</strong> This is an adverb meaning "really" or "truly" in English. It’s used here to intensify the adjective that follows.</div><div><strong>amusant</strong> This is the masculine singular form of the adjective "amusant", which means "fun" or "amusing" in English. If the subject is feminine, it would be "amusante". It describes the potential enjoyment of the situation or activity being referred to.</div>','<h2>Pourquoi est-ce qu’ils parlent comme ça?</h2><div><strong>Pourquoi</strong> This is the French word for "why". It’s used to ask about the reason or purpose behind something.</div><div><strong>est-ce que</strong> This is a French phrase that is used to form a question. It literally translates to "is it that" in English, but a natural English equivalent would simply be the question format using "do" or "does".</div><div><strong>ils</strong> This is the third-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "they" in English. It’s used as the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>parlent</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "parler", which means "to speak" or "to talk".</div><div><strong>comme</strong> This word translates to "like" or "as" in English. It is used to draw a comparison or describe a manner of doing something.</div><div><strong>ça</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun that translates to "that" or "it" in English. It’s used here to refer to the manner of speaking.</div>','<h2>D’accord. Nous allons nous occuper de ça.</h2><div><strong>D’accord</strong> This is a French expression that translates to "okay" or "agreed". It signifies understanding or acceptance of a point.</div><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>allons</strong> This is the first-person plural present tense form of the verb "aller", which means "to go". In this context, it is used to form the future tense along with an infinitive, creating the meaning "we are going (to do something)".</div><div><strong>nous occuper de</strong> This phrase combines the reflexive pronoun "nous" and the verb "occuper", which means "to take care of". The "de" at the end indicates what is being taken care of. Together, "nous occuper de" means "to take care of" or "to deal with" in a reflexive form, indicating that the subject is taking care of something.</div><div><strong>ça</strong> This is a French demonstrative pronoun that translates to "that" or "it" in English. It is the object of the sentence and refers to something that has been previously mentioned or is understood from the context.</div>','<h2>D’accord, je vais faire ça tout de suite.</h2><div><strong>D’accord</strong> This is a French expression meaning "OK" or "Alright". It signifies agreement or consent to a proposal or situation.</div><div><strong>je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject pronoun used here.</div><div><strong>vais</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "aller", which means "to go". However, in this context it is used as part of the near future construction, which in English translates to "I am going to" do something.</div><div><strong>faire</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "faire", which means "to do" or "to make". It is used here in combination with "vais" to form the near future tense.</div><div><strong>ça</strong> This pronoun means "that" in English. It refers to a previously mentioned or understood object, action, or situation.</div><div><strong>tout de suite</strong> This phrase translates to "right away" or "immediately" in English, indicating that the action will happen without delay.</div>','<h2>Vers huit heures, ça vous va?</h2><div><strong>Vers</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "around" or "towards". It indicates an approximation of time in this context.</div><div><strong>huit heures</strong> This phrase means "eight o’clock". "Huit" is the number 8 in French, and "heures" is the word for hours, referring to the time of day.</div><div><strong>ça</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun in French which translates to "that" or "it". In this context, it refers to the proposed time of "around eight o’clock".</div><div><strong>vous</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It can indicate a formal address or refer to more than one person.</div><div><strong>va?</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense of the verb "aller", which means "to go". In this context, it’s used to ask if the proposed time "works" or "suits" the person being asked. When combined with "ça", it forms the expression "ça vous va?" which means "does that work for you?" or "is that okay with you?".</div>','<h2>Vous avez pensé à ça toute la journée.</h2><div><strong>Vous</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It can indicate formal address or refer to more than one person. Here, it acts as the subject pronoun in the sentence.</div><div><strong>avez</strong> This word is the present tense form of "avoir" for the second-person formal or plural, which means "to have". In this sentence, it’s used as an auxiliary verb to form the past tense (Passé Composé) with the main verb "pensé".</div><div><strong>pensé</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "penser", which means "to think". When used with the auxiliary verb "avoir", it forms the Passé Composé to denote past action.</div><div><strong>à</strong> This preposition in French can translate to "to" in English, among other uses. In this sentence, it connects the verb "pensé" to the object "ça" to indicate what was thought about.</div><div><strong>ça</strong> This is a casual form of "cela", which means "that" in English. It is used as the direct object in the sentence, representing the thing that was thought about.</div><div><strong>toute</strong> This word is an adjective that translates to "all" or "entire". In this sentence, it modifies "la journée" to express the idea of the entire day.</div><div><strong>la</strong> This is the definite article "the" in English, used here for the feminine singular noun "journée".</div><div><strong>journée</strong> This is a feminine singular noun that means "day" in English. It signifies the time period that the thinking occurred over.</div>','<h2>Ils ne devraient pas se disputer comme ça.</h2><div><strong>Ils</strong> This is the third-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "they" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence, referring to a group of people.</div><div><strong>ne</strong> This word is used as part of a negation in French. Along with "pas", it negates the verb of the sentence. In spoken French, "ne" is often dropped.</div><div><strong>devraient</strong> This is the third-person plural conditional tense form of the verb "devoir", which means "must" or "should". It suggests what the subject should or ought not to do.</div><div><strong>pas</strong> This is the second part of the negation in French, used here with "ne" to negate the verb "devraient". It is also commonly used alone in spoken French to indicate negation.</div><div><strong>se</strong> This is a reflexive pronoun, which indicates that the verb is being done by the subjects to themselves. It is part of the reflexive verb "se disputer", meaning "to argue" or "to quarrel".</div><div><strong>disputer</strong> This is the infinitive form of the reflexive verb "se disputer", which means "to argue" or "to quarrel". In this sentence, it is used in the context of the subjects arguing with each other.</div><div><strong>comme ça</strong> This phrase translates to "like that" in English. It is an adverbial phrase that describes the manner in which the subjects are arguing.</div>','<h2>Et à part ça, quels sont tes loisirs?</h2><div><strong>Et</strong> This word translates as "and" in English. Used as a conjunction, it connects phrases or clauses in a sentence.</div><div><strong>à</strong> This French preposition means "to" in English, and it is often used in various contexts and phrases.</div><div><strong>part</strong> This word can often mean "part" or "aside" in English. In this context, "à part" together with "et" translates to "apart from that" or "besides that".</div><div><strong>ça</strong> This is the informal pronoun for "that" in English. It’s the contraction of "cela", which is more formal.</div><div><strong>quels</strong> This is the plural masculine form of "quel", which is an interrogative adjective in French meaning "which" or "what" in English. It is used here to modify the noun "loisirs" (hobbies).</div><div><strong>sont</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "être", meaning "to be" in English. In this context, it translates to "are".</div><div><strong>tes</strong> This is the possessive adjective "your" in English for the second-person singular. It indicates that the "loisirs" (hobbies) belong to the person being spoken to.</div><div><strong>loisirs?</strong> This is a French noun for "hobbies" or "leisure activities" in English. The plural form indicates multiple activities or interests.</div>','<h2>Ça m’ennuie que tu t’inquiètes autant.</h2><div><strong>Ça</strong> This is the French pronoun that translates to "it" or "that" in English. It’s used as the subject of the sentence here to refer to an unspecified thing or situation.</div><div><strong>m’ennuie</strong> This is a contraction of "me" and "ennuie". "Me" is a first-person singular object pronoun, equivalent to "me" in English. "Ennuie" is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "ennuyer", which means "to bother" or "to annoy". In this case, the verb in combination with "me" means "bothers me" or "annoys me".</div><div><strong>que</strong> This word translates to "that" in English. It’s a conjunction used to introduce a subordinate clause.</div><div><strong>tu</strong> Here, "tu" is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It’s the subject of the subordinate clause.</div><div><strong>t’inquiètes</strong> A contraction of "te" and "inquiètes"; "te" is a second-person singular object pronoun, similar to "yourself" in English. "Inquiètes" is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "inquiéter", which means "to worry". Together, they mean "you worry" or "worry yourself".</div><div><strong>autant</strong> This word translates to "so much" or "as much" in English. It’s an adverb that describes the extent or degree of the worrying.</div>','<h2>Ne fais pas ça, c’est trop dangereux.</h2><div><strong>Ne</strong> This is one part of the French negation structure, typically used before the verb. It’s used to negate a statement. The complete negation is formed with "ne... pas".</div><div><strong>fais</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "faire", which means "to do" or "to make". It’s an imperative form, giving a command or a request.</div><div><strong>pas</strong> This is the second part of the French negation structure that surrounds the verb, completing the negation started by "ne". Together, "ne...pas" translates to "do not" or "don’t" in this context.</div><div><strong>ça</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun meaning "that". It refers to an action or object that has been previously mentioned or is understood from context.</div><div><strong>c’est</strong> This is a contraction of "ce" (it/this/that) and "est" (third-person singular present tense form of "être", meaning "is"). "C’est" translates to "it is" or "this is".</div><div><strong>trop</strong> This is an adverb meaning "too" in English. It is used here to intensify the adjective that follows.</div><div><strong>dangereux</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "dangerous". Here, it’s used in its masculine singular form to describe a situation or action as being hazardous.</div>','<h2>Je suis désolé, vous ne pouvez pas emporter ça.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>suis</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". Here, it’s used to form the compound tense which is expressing that the speaker is sorry or apologetic.</div><div><strong>désolé</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "sorry" or "apologetic". It matches the masculine singular form to agree with the speaker. If the speaker were female, it would be "désolée".</div><div><strong>vous</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It can be used for both formal and informal address, and for one person or several people.</div><div><strong>ne ... pas</strong> This is the negative construction in French, which wraps around a verb to negate it. In English, this would be equivalent to placing "not" after the auxiliary verb.</div><div><strong>pouvez</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural form of the verb "pouvoir", which means "to be able" or "can". It indicates possibility or permission.</div><div><strong>emporter</strong> This is an infinitive verb in French which translates to "to take away" or "to carry out" in English.</div><div><strong>ça</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun in French, meaning "that". It is used here to refer to something that has been mentioned or is understood from the context.</div>','<h2>Ah oui? On va regarder ça. Asseyez-vous...</h2><div><strong>Ah</strong> This is an interjection in French used to express a realization or surprise, similar to "Oh" in English.</div><div><strong>oui</strong> This is the French word for "yes". It’s often used as a response to confirm something or to show agreement.</div><div><strong>On</strong> This is the French indefinite pronoun that is equivalent to "one" in English but is often used to mean "we" or "you" in a general sense.</div><div><strong>va</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "aller", which means "to go". In this context, it’s part of a near future construction ("on va" + infinitive) equivalent to the English "going to" for indicating a future action.</div><div><strong>regarder</strong> This is the infinitive form of a French verb meaning "to watch" or "to look at".</div><div><strong>ça</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun in French that means "that" or "it". It stands for the thing that is going to be looked at or watched.</div><div><strong>Asseyez-vous</strong> This is the reflexive command form of "s’asseoir", which means "to sit down". The "-vous" suffix is used to show that the command is formal or plural.</div>','<h2>Vous allez à Montréal aujourd’hui, c’est ça?</h2><div><strong>Vous</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It’s used to formally address one person or to address more than one person.</div><div><strong>allez</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural present tense form of the verb "aller", which means "to go". In this sentence, it indicates the action or intention of going somewhere.</div><div><strong>à</strong> This is a French preposition that generally means "to" when describing a direction or destination.</div><div><strong>Montréal</strong> Montréal is a city in the Canadian province of Quebec. In this sentence, it is the destination toward which the action of going is directed.</div><div><strong>aujourd’hui</strong> This is the French word for "today". It indicates the time when the action is taking place.</div><div><strong>c’est ça?</strong> This phrase is a French colloquial expression that translates to "right?" or "isn’t it?" It’s used at the end of a sentence to confirm or seek confirmation about the preceding statement.</div><div><strong>c’</strong> This is a contraction of "ce", which is a demonstrative pronoun in French. It translates to "it" or "this" in English.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be".</div><div><strong>ça</strong> This translates to "that" in English and refers back to the statement that was made.</div>'];definitions = definitions.concat(defBonus);